course: dictionary
lesson: 9730
title: Sunglasses and staying safe
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Put on your sunglasses» tells you to wear them. Sunglasses are glasses with dark or tinted lenses that protect your eyes from bright sunlight. The word is always plural, so you say a pair of sunglasses.
explain: The words around a word give you clues. «UV rays can damage the eyes too, so sunglasses should carry a UV-protection mark.» The words damage and protection tell you that sunglasses are for keeping your eyes safe from harm.
explain: Read the whole sentence before you decide. «Dark lenses alone don't mean better protection» tells you that a dark tint is not enough. Look for the UV-protection mark, not just how dark the lenses are.
fact: Sunglasses means glasses with dark or tinted lenses that protect your eyes from bright sunlight.
fact: Sunglasses is always plural, so say «a pair of sunglasses».
fact: «Slip, Slop, Slap, Seek, Slide» is a sun safety rule from Australia and New Zealand: slip on a shirt, slop on sun cream, slap on a hat, seek shade, slide on sunglasses.
fact: Good sunglasses block UV light, which can harm your eyes. Snow and water reflect sunlight, so sunglasses are needed there too.
fact: Never look straight at the Sun, even with sunglasses. «Sunglasses are not enough, only proper eclipse glasses are safe» during an eclipse.
exercise: «In Australia children learn to slide on sunglasses.» What does slide on sunglasses mean? | *Put sunglasses on to protect your eyes | Take your sunglasses off and fold them | Slide down a hill wearing sunglasses | Clean your sunglasses with a cloth
exercise: Ella says sunglasses are only for looking cool. What is their main job? | To make the Sun safe to stare at | To make the sky look darker | *To protect your eyes from bright sunlight | To keep your hair dry
exercise: Find and copy the word that means harm in «UV rays can damage the eyes too». | sunglasses | eyes | *damage | rays
test: «Dark lenses alone don't mean better protection.» What does this tell you? | Dark lenses always block all UV light | *Dark lenses are not enough, so look for a UV-protection mark | Only the price of sunglasses matters | The darker the lenses, the safer they are
test: Looking at the Sun during an eclipse can damage your eyes for good. Which glasses are safe to use? | Your normal school glasses | Glasses with no lenses at all | *Proper eclipse glasses | Sunglasses, even very dark ones
exam: Dad says, «Sunglasses are all you need when you look at the Sun.» Is he right? | Yes, but only on snow | *No, sunglasses do not make looking at the Sun safe | Yes, dark sunglasses make the Sun safe to look at | Yes, if you also wear a hat
